    "lore": "When the Italian Empire expanded at a breakneck pace during World War Two, few among the fascist leadership stopped to consider the fact that some of the newly conquered territories would have been more trouble than they were worth. In the Levant, Italian rule was constantly marked by fierce resistance by native Arabs, and the plans to counterbalance them with Jewish immigrants backfired as Zionist groups took up arms against Italy as well. Dalla Chiesa, the new young Governor, adopted a new strategy - attempting to negotiate with moderate of both camps, which fostered an uneasy peace between the Italians, Haganah and Fatah. The threat of Turkey looms on the horizon, and it might be the only thing capable of uniting the peoples of the Levant, or to once and for all doom the region to chaos."
